{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015jrE6MmrnVPanJJqMEeYwi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive picture. Let me generate the detailed predicted results for each planned experiment under the \"best\" tier. The experiment plan lists these as \"planned\":\n\n1. plan_001 - real_data_dif_mmlu\n2. plan_002 - rl_robustness_validation\n3. plan_003a - constat_perturbation_eval\n4. plan_003b - dvd_generation_variance_eval\n5. plan_003c - ensemble_fusion\n6. plan_004 - cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n7. plan_005 - bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n\n\n8. plan_006 - item_generation_calibration\n9. plan_007 - cumulative_bayesian_dif\n10. plan_008 - graceful_degradation_analysis\n11. plan_009 - item_focused_trees\n12. plan_010 - architecture_stratified_dif\n13. plan_011 - semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n\nNow I'm cross-referencing against the registry to see which ones have already been executed. Several show completion status—plan_001, plan_001b, plan_004, plan_011, and plan_012 all came back negative, while plan_011 succeeded, and plan_013 appears to be partially recorded. I see there's a discrepancy between what's marked as \"planned\" in the experiment_plan section and what's already been executed in the registry. The user is asking me to generate predicted results for all experiments listed as planned, using the \"best\" tier definition. For experiments that have already run, I should use their actual results as the basis but frame them within the best-tier context. For the ones not yet executed, I need to imagine what the optimal outcomes would look like.\n\nIn the best tier, all four claims survive peer review intact, the framing is recognized as genuine, and the meta-critique lands effectively. So I'm generating comprehensive YAML output for all 13 experiments where the results support the narrative without requiring any softening or caveats.\n\nFor the completed experiment (plan_001), the actual numbers hold up: the 31.3% C% value, the enrichment factor around 0.96 confirming the meta-critique, domain specificity correlation of 0.307, and the sanity check at 0.18%. For the unexecuted experiments starting with plan_002, I'm imagining what robust validation results would show across different training approaches...\n\nDIF remains stable across SFT and RL variants with high Jaccard similarity, while ConStat and Min-K% show degradation on reasoning-RL models. The remaining dropped experiments would show ConStat with moderate power on non-RL models but limited coverage compared to DIF, DVD catching variant contamination but missing verbatim matches, and a 2-of-3 ensemble providing full spectrum coverage. For the cross-benchmark work, the correlation coefficient of -0.478 is accepted as suggestive evidence.\n\nThe bifactor IRT approach fits well enough to anchor selection with equated rankings above 0.96, LLM-generated items achieve difficulty correlations of 0.70 after calibration, and cumulative Bayesian DIF reaches 0.70 power after three administrations. Validity degrades gracefully up to 30% item replacement, and item-focused trees continue to show promise.\n\nNow I'm seeing that the covariate DIF analysis confirms the temporal pattern I was tracking. The architecture stratified DIF is complete with decoder-only at 99.98% and perfect Jaccard overlap, while the semi-synthetic contamination validation shows a dose-response relationship with ΔTPR gains of 0.508 at p=0.5. I'm ready to write out the complete YAML configuration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2506,"cache_read_input_tokens":31147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2506},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57957a